1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,120
Today's mission: Unbelievable.

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:13,400
This operation is brought to you by Kaede Hondo, who's looking forward to eating eel four years from now, and someone who will surely still be working as a comedian four years from now...

3
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:19,860
...Hantaro Tenshin. It's a program that might not even still be on the air four years from now.

4
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:26,360
Kaede Hondo and Hantaro Tenshin's Hondo Landing Operation.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,860
Good evening, I'm Kaede Hondo.

6
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:35,700
Hello, I'm Hantaro Tenshin.

7
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:43,720
This week's introduction was sent in by listener Gonbohori: “The radio show might be over within the next four years.”

8
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:45,000
You never know, right?

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,540
Four more years would make fifteen years.

10
00:00:48,540 --> 00:00:51,320
It would be amazing if we lasted fifteen years.

11
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:53,260
Oh no, maybe we won't make it.

12
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:56,740
It would be amazing if we did. We're running out of energy, aren't we?

13
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:57,500
I'm starving.

14
00:00:58,340 --> 00:00:59,420
Fifteen years is already...

15
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:01,420
What, is that really so long?

16
00:01:01,420 --> 00:01:12,780
On Bunka Broadcasting, Golden Radio has been running about that long, hasn't it? Yeah, it has been on for fifteen years. Wow, I see.

17
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:16,460
Right, of course. There must be plenty of shows like that.

18
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:20,920
So fifteen years really is possible? Apparently.

19
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:21,640
I see.

20
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:26,660
If we make it fifteen years, I can celebrate turning fifty here.

21
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:28,660
Oh wow. Let's celebrate in style.

22
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:29,400
And then...

23
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:33,860
Maybe we'll order Uber Eats here and let you eat whatever you want.

24
00:01:33,860 --> 00:01:35,680
That's half a century.

25
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:37,020
It really is—Hantaro's half-century.

26
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:39,640
Amazing. Fifty is already in sight.

27
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:40,360
Yikes.

28
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:41,260
Wonderful.

29
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:43,760
So that's roughly five years from now?

30
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:46,660
Well, four more years, actually.

31
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:48,160
I'll turn fifty in four years.

32
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:49,640
And I'll be thirty-four.

33
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:50,280
Yeah.

34
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:53,540
Seriously, we're starting to show our age.

35
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:53,940
Fifty.

36
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:57,340
The other day I happened to see Hondo-san at another job.

37
00:01:57,340 --> 00:02:03,360
I had a chance to meet her, and she was wearing this rather elegant dress.

38
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:06,040
Me? Right—the outfit they prepared for me.

39
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:21,380
I happened to see her from a short distance away, looking so grown-up in that outfit, and honestly felt like one of her relatives.

40
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:23,240
That is definitely a relative's way of looking at her. Exactly.

41
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:29,020
But there were makeup artists and stylists there. I don't have anything like that dress in my own wardrobe.

42
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:30,980
Right. You looked incredibly beautiful.

43
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:31,460
Exactly.

44
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:42,340
It was that sleeveless dress. It made me realize, ‘Wow, I guess I really do see you that way.’ And I suppose from here on out, I'll have more and more moments like that.

45
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:47,220
Please keep watching over me. Likewise—I'd like you to watch me turn fifty too.

46
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:51,140
I'm glad you can raise your arm again. You talked about it on the show last week.

47
00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:55,360
Yes, my frozen shoulder has recovered enough that I can raise my arm about eighty-five percent of the way now.

48
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:56,540
Good, good.

49
00:02:56,540 --> 00:02:58,940
It reminded me that people really do recover.

50
00:02:58,940 --> 00:02:59,440
Yeah.

51
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:03,300
Just look after your health. My neuralgia has settled down considerably too.

52
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:06,120
That's good. Looking back, you had a lot going on.

53
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:10,640
I really had a lot of health problems. You've been through quite a bit, then.

54
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:12,480
Yes, I've recovered considerably.

55
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:13,080
I'm glad.

56
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:16,380
Now you can all watch the powerful me again.

57
00:03:16,380 --> 00:03:22,580
Whoa! Here we go. “The powerful me”—that's an old-fashioned expression.

58
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:26,980
Anyway, we read this week's objective.

59
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:37,300
Perhaps we'll talk more about that later. I'm looking forward to finding out. And now, Hondo Landing Operation begins!

60
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:45,540
This program is sponsored by Anime Blast.

61
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:48,020
So this is Anime Blast.

62
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:52,800
The place where we landed was the legendary continent of Anime Blast.

63
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:57,280
Here, we will discover new ways to enjoy anime.

64
00:03:57,280 --> 00:03:59,540
Our dream is far from over.

65
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:00,820
Let's go, Hantaro!

66
00:04:00,820 --> 00:04:04,380
Search for “Anime Blast” to see what happens next. Search!

67
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:09,600
That's old-fashioned. Are you ready? I'm Kaede Hondo.

68
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:10,580
I'm Hantaro Tenshin.

69
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:12,720
Hondo Landing Operation.

70
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:15,620
I'm Kaede Hondo.

71
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:16,780
I'm Hantaro Tenshin.

72
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:19,680
So, listen.

73
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:23,800
Anyone who listens every week probably already knows this.

74
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:32,960
As we mentioned at the start, we keep talking about eating eel four years from now. Ultimately, that means we should all go out for dinner together.

75
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:35,260
Exactly. We've been saying it for ages.

76
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:37,620
Yes, we have.

77
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:40,300
So let's hurry up and do it.

78
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:41,800
Let's do it.

79
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:44,000
The thing is, with this group...

80
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,460
To be honest, the writer and director are homebodies. They never take action.

81
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:52,760
I know. I'll say it plainly: you don't do anything.

82
00:04:52,760 --> 00:05:03,720
You seem almost indifferent to whether we have this dinner, but we want to go. We were talking about that after recording the show.

83
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:07,820
As we were taking the elevator down to the first floor, Hondo-san said...

84
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:10,280
“We really should finally make this happen.”

85
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:13,420
So Hondo-san and I decided...

86
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:28,340
She told me that the two of us should take the initiative and settle on a date. If we did that, the writer, director, and producer would surely join us.

87
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:50,320
All right, I'll get the LINE group moving. You know the Chouseisan scheduling tool? I sent a poll to the LINE group so we could settle on a date.

88
00:05:50,320 --> 00:06:09,700
I marked the days in October when I was free and asked Hondo-san and everyone else to fill it out so we could find a date. I made and sent the poll two or three weeks ago.

89
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:19,480
About two weeks ago. I opened that Chouseisan poll just now.

90
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:29,660
None of you had answered it! Give me a break!

91
00:06:29,660 --> 00:06:37,920
The whole time it kept showing only one respondent.

92
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:45,280
Only one person. I'm not joking. And who was that one person? Me.

93
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:49,460
I'm genuinely sorry.

94
00:06:49,460 --> 00:07:06,660
I forgot because things were hectic—and, you know, I went to New York. Don't act all affected! Sorry! “Sorry”? Knock it off!

95
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:19,300
Seriously, you should have seen my face go pale after I cheerfully wondered when everyone was free. I did open it once, but thought October was still quite far away.

96
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:25,080
So you decided to leave it for later? Why? We chose October precisely because people would still have open dates!

97
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:28,480
The director is no better. “I don't know my schedule until shortly beforehand.”

98
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:35,080
There it is—he escaped! Don't turn it into a poetic excuse. Wait a moment!

99
00:07:35,080 --> 00:07:40,260
That's a great escape. Let me question everyone individually. The writer thought October was too far away too?

100
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:44,460
I thought we'd have a programming-season change before then. Ah, the schedule reshuffle—fair point.

101
00:07:44,460 --> 00:07:48,460
Since it's October, more work might still be added.

102
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:55,140
By mid-September I'd have a clearer view of my October schedule. I see.

103
00:07:55,140 --> 00:08:01,980
I understand the reasoning, but you could at least have written that in the LINE chat. Anyway, fine.

104
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:05,880
Director? Director? Yes?

105
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:12,420
Why are you only finding out now? What were you looking at? What happened to the LINE group? Everyone had marked the message as read!

106
00:08:12,420 --> 00:08:15,580
What did you think it was? How did you not know?

107
00:08:15,580 --> 00:08:18,500
I dislike leaving unread notifications, though I understand the impulse.

108
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:22,900
It happens. You open everything. You shouldn't, though.

109
00:08:22,900 --> 00:08:24,660
And what was Hondo-san's excuse?

110
00:08:24,660 --> 00:08:29,900
I saw something had arrived, replied “Thank you!” with an exclamation mark, and planned to look at it later.

111
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:33,600
Then it got buried beneath my other LINE chats and I forgot.

112
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:38,020
Then I went to New York. Is “I went to New York” becoming your universal excuse?

113
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:46,300
October was so far away that I had no idea what my schedule would be. Wasn't October chosen because that made it easier to adjust our schedules?

114
00:08:46,300 --> 00:08:48,300
It isn't some poetic excuse, honestly!

115
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:52,900
It isn't! This really is unbelievable. Mukai-kun, do you want to go instead?

116
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:53,400
Me?

117
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:54,000
What do you think?

118
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:54,500
Shall we go?

119
00:08:54,500 --> 00:08:56,200
This is really getting on my nerves.

120
00:08:56,200 --> 00:08:59,400
Fine, I'll do it later. Though I'm busy today too.

121
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:05,900
I'll do it another day. Enough, seriously. I'm done with all of you.

122
00:09:05,900 --> 00:09:08,600
I'm deleting the Chouseisan poll—erasing it from existence.

123
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:12,500
Don't delete it! Please! I'm sorry!

124
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:19,100
Seriously. That poetic-excuse music has been playing this whole time. Anyway, I went to New York, and I...

125
00:09:19,300 --> 00:09:23,300
And I went to Thailand—khop khun kha.

126
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:24,300
Khop khun kha?

127
00:09:24,300 --> 00:09:27,300
I went abroad too.

128
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:28,300
Khop khun kha.

129
00:09:28,300 --> 00:09:29,800
Yes—sawasdee kha, khop khun kha.

130
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:32,800
Did we get an email about both trips?

131
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:35,800
Yes. This is from listener “Dim Fluorescent Light.” Thank you.

132
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:38,300
“Hondo-san, Hantaro-san, good evening.”

133
00:09:38,300 --> 00:09:42,300
“Congratulations to both of you on going international.” Thank you.

134
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:44,300
“Hondo-san, you were in New York for an event, correct?”

135
00:09:44,300 --> 00:09:45,300
Yes.

136
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:48,300
“I could tell from your posts on X that you were enjoying yourself...”

137
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:51,300
“...but I'd be happy to hear more about it.” I already talked about it.

138
00:09:51,300 --> 00:09:57,300
“And on the August 20 episode, Hantaro-san only said that he was looking forward to going overseas.”

139
00:09:57,300 --> 00:10:00,300
“If you don't mind, we'd like to hear about your trip.”

140
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:02,300
Sure, I can talk about it. Khop khun kha.

141
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:06,300
Sorry—is “khop khun kha” Thai?

142
00:10:06,300 --> 00:10:08,300
Sorry, where was “khop khun kha” from again?

143
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:10,300
Thailand. Sorry.

144
00:10:10,300 --> 00:10:15,300
That's right. I took some time off and went to Thailand.

145
00:10:15,300 --> 00:10:17,300
Lucky you! Yeah.

146
00:10:17,300 --> 00:10:18,300
Did you get to take a proper trip?

147
00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:21,300
Yes, though by “proper” I mean two nights and three days.

148
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:23,300
So it was entirely personal, not work?

149
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:32,300
Mostly. Sakura Inagaki Saki, a junior comedian from our agency who does an Asuka impression, now lives in Thailand.

150
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:35,300
I see. She has moved there now.

151
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:50,300
I was also scouting the possibility of holding an event there someday, but it was essentially a vacation. Thai food really suits my taste.

152
00:10:50,300 --> 00:10:53,300
Really? I love Thailand. Sounds great.

153
00:10:53,300 --> 00:11:07,300
We went to a very famous place, something like a Chinese restaurant, and ate and drank our fill. Even with the weak yen, it would probably have cost twice as much in Japan.

154
00:11:07,300 --> 00:11:14,300
Really? So it was fairly cheap. Yes, and the city was lively—there were places like night markets.

155
00:11:14,300 --> 00:11:21,300
They sold all kinds of things. At the night market there was even crocodile meat.

156
00:11:21,300 --> 00:11:22,300
Wow, incredible.

157
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:43,300
They also had wonderfully tropical mango juice. Everything felt fresh. We ate delicious food, visited some sights, and had an enjoyable, ordinary vacation. That's basically the whole story.

158
00:11:43,300 --> 00:11:44,300
No, wait—that can't be all, can it?

159
00:11:44,300 --> 00:11:45,300
What about the incident?

160
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:46,300
Yes?

161
00:11:46,300 --> 00:11:47,300
What was the “unbelievable” part?

162
00:11:47,300 --> 00:11:49,300
The “unbelievable” part was about the radio staff ignoring the scheduling poll.

163
00:11:49,300 --> 00:11:54,300
Oh, that—not the trip.

164
00:11:54,300 --> 00:11:57,300
I thought your Thailand story was building to some dramatic excuse. No, it was simply a vacation.

165
00:11:57,300 --> 00:11:59,300
Really?

166
00:11:59,300 --> 00:12:01,300
But isn't that story rather shallow if it's meant to be dramatic?

167
00:12:01,300 --> 00:12:02,300
Hmm?

168
00:12:02,300 --> 00:12:07,300
There must be more to it. He simply won't tell us unless we ask.

169
00:12:07,300 --> 00:12:09,300
So we have to ask? We have to draw it out of him?

170
00:12:09,300 --> 00:12:10,300
Who did you go with?

171
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:12,300
I really have to be prompted?

172
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:13,300
Sorry?

173
00:12:13,300 --> 00:12:15,920
Sorry.

174
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:21,460
I went with a younger broadcast writer from my agency who's the same age as me.

175
00:12:21,460 --> 00:12:24,280
A man.

176
00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:25,700
Just the two of us.

177
00:12:25,700 --> 00:12:30,380
He speaks English.

178
00:12:30,380 --> 00:12:32,440
That must have helped.

179
00:12:32,440 --> 00:12:36,680
It helped enormously. English isn't an official language of Thailand...

180
00:12:36,680 --> 00:12:39,180
...which is unusual within Asia.

181
00:12:39,180 --> 00:12:45,340
But we were in Bangkok, and in a city like Bangkok people understand English.

182
00:12:45,340 --> 00:12:48,660
So he was incredibly helpful.

183
00:12:48,660 --> 00:12:59,820
Inagaki Saki joined us too and speaks some Thai, so we had no trouble at all. She took us to good restaurants.

184
00:12:59,820 --> 00:13:01,760
But honestly...

185
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:04,800
The biggest problem with the trip was...

186
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:13,020
...that nothing story-worthy had happened. I began to worry about that. So it really was uneventful? But enjoying yourself is a good thing.

187
00:13:13,020 --> 00:13:17,100
It was great as a vacation, but right at the very end...

188
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:22,720
Our return flight was at about 1 a.m.

189
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:26,620
Five or six hours before the flight, the writer traveling with me said...

190
00:13:26,620 --> 00:13:30,500
“Hantaro-san, nothing has happened on this trip.”

191
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:33,520
“Are you going to be all right?” That was when it suddenly struck me.

192
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:38,060
What was I going to do? I had announced on Hondo Landing Operation that I was going abroad.

193
00:13:38,060 --> 00:13:42,080
If I hadn't said that, an uneventful trip would have been fine. But I had said it.

194
00:13:42,080 --> 00:13:44,420
So I wondered what to do.

195
00:13:44,420 --> 00:13:54,320
We looked for somewhere to go. We almost rejected one place for being too far away, but found an event at an anime-song DJ club.

196
00:13:54,320 --> 00:13:56,820
So we decided to try it.

197
00:13:56,820 --> 00:13:59,580
It was some distance away...

198
00:13:59,580 --> 00:14:04,460
...but we took a taxi there. The listing said it was here.

199
00:14:04,460 --> 00:14:07,380
The address matched the place.

200
00:14:07,380 --> 00:14:12,060
It said it opened at 7 p.m., and it was already after seven, so we thought we could go in.

201
00:14:12,060 --> 00:14:15,080
But when we looked around, the entire building was dark.

202
00:14:15,080 --> 00:14:16,980
Pitch-black. What was going on?

203
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:21,420
We thought, “It's dark—what?”

204
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:25,800
We weren't sure what to do, but decided to go upstairs.

205
00:14:25,800 --> 00:14:29,840
Apparently the fifth floor was supposed to be the place.

206
00:14:29,840 --> 00:14:33,040
We went up, but the fifth floor was completely dark too.

207
00:14:33,040 --> 00:14:34,520
Why?

208
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:38,840
We found what seemed to be the anime-song club.

209
00:14:38,840 --> 00:14:40,520
And on its door...

210
00:14:40,520 --> 00:14:48,780
...was a photo of baseball manager Tatsunori Hara. I don't know baseball well enough to tell when it was taken.

211
00:14:48,780 --> 00:14:53,500
It was a photo of him doing a fist bump. We wondered how old it was.

212
00:14:53,500 --> 00:14:57,860
There was a photo posted there, but was the club actually operating?

213
00:14:57,860 --> 00:15:00,700
We wondered what to do.

214
00:15:00,700 --> 00:15:06,800
We went back downstairs because it was dark and decided to search again somewhere brighter.

215
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:17,100
While the two of us were looking it up downstairs, someone said, “Excuse me.” She was an unmistakably blonde...

216
00:15:17,100 --> 00:15:27,500
Imagine the stereotypical blonde beauty. She said “Excuse me,” dressed in an extremely glamorous outfit...

217
00:15:27,500 --> 00:15:36,880
...with very short shorts. I wondered what she wanted. I couldn't speak English, but the writer could.

218
00:15:36,880 --> 00:15:41,760
They began speaking rapidly while I stood there wondering what they were saying.

219
00:15:41,760 --> 00:15:49,500
I hid behind the writer, curious about their conversation. Apparently, she was visiting from overseas...

220
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:53,520
...from somewhere other than Thailand.

221
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:55,460
I think she was from North America.

222
00:15:55,460 --> 00:15:58,980
She had come to Thailand on vacation.

223
00:15:58,980 --> 00:16:13,860
She loved anime and had searched for an anime event. That search brought her to this same place, for exactly the same reason as us.

224
00:16:13,860 --> 00:16:18,480
Exactly the same. She was also confused because the building was dark.

225
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:21,100
She had seen the two of us go upstairs...

226
00:16:21,100 --> 00:16:28,040
...and then come back down, so she wanted to ask what was happening. We explained that we were in the same situation.

227
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:30,400
Then the two of them continued talking in English.

228
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:33,140
They discussed what to do and offered to search for more information.

229
00:16:33,140 --> 00:16:35,960
They even laughed together. I felt left out.

230
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:38,440
I couldn't understand anything at all.

231
00:16:38,440 --> 00:16:42,300
All I could do was laugh along about one second after they did. You understood? No, I didn't.

232
00:16:42,300 --> 00:16:59,000
After everyone got excited talking, we decided to go upstairs once more and knock. Nobody answered. Meanwhile, the blonde woman...

233
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:10,160
...looked it up and called the club. You know how Google sometimes says a place opens at seven even though it doesn't?

234
00:17:10,160 --> 00:17:11,480
That was exactly what had happened.

235
00:17:11,480 --> 00:17:14,000
She called them and learned...

236
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:15,700
“We open at nine.”

237
00:17:15,700 --> 00:17:16,500
Oh, I see.

238
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:18,620
So there were two hours to wait.

239
00:17:18,620 --> 00:17:22,060
You waited two hours? From that point...

240
00:17:22,060 --> 00:17:28,000
...it was a little under ninety minutes. We realized we'd have to wait.

241
00:17:28,700 --> 00:17:32,280
We thought there was nothing else to do and started to leave.

242
00:17:32,280 --> 00:17:44,060
We were about to part ways, but I was still worried that I had no story from the trip. The blonde woman planned to kill time somewhere for ninety minutes.

243
00:17:44,060 --> 00:17:47,700
Right. I thought, “This is my chance.”

244
00:17:47,700 --> 00:17:51,140
She had said she loved anime, so I approached her.

245
00:17:51,140 --> 00:17:54,460
Stop!

246
00:17:54,460 --> 00:17:56,340
That's frightening!

247
00:17:56,340 --> 00:17:57,500
Don't do it!

248
00:17:57,500 --> 00:18:01,840
It happened so suddenly...

249
00:18:01,840 --> 00:18:11,340
...that I couldn't think of any words. Imagine that my English is at first-year middle-school level. So I said, “Girl...”

250
00:18:11,340 --> 00:18:17,180
“Girl, girl...” There was a bar farther inside...

251
00:18:17,180 --> 00:18:23,620
I could see the bar, but I couldn't even remember the word “bar.”

252
00:18:23,620 --> 00:18:26,100
Seriously. “Girl...”

253
00:18:26,100 --> 00:18:31,000
“Go... next...”

254
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:36,800
“Here...” I had no idea what I was saying. It was hopeless.

255
00:18:36,800 --> 00:18:41,280
I was completely panicking. She looked at me as if to ask what I was saying.

256
00:18:41,280 --> 00:18:43,880
I knew this was bad.

257
00:18:43,880 --> 00:18:45,580
“Uh...”

258
00:18:45,580 --> 00:18:50,080
“I'm... uh...”

259
00:18:50,080 --> 00:18:52,880
“Japanese famous...”

260
00:18:52,880 --> 00:18:54,640
“Anime event...” I'm impressed you remembered that much.

261
00:18:54,640 --> 00:18:55,220
“MC.”

262
00:18:55,220 --> 00:19:01,020
You remembered all that even though you couldn't remember “bar”? It's a stock phrase for me.

263
00:19:01,020 --> 00:19:08,360
When comedians travel abroad, there's a routine where they say, “I'm a famous comedian.” This was the anime version.

264
00:19:08,520 --> 00:19:09,520
Oh!

265
00:19:09,520 --> 00:19:10,520
How about that?

266
00:19:10,520 --> 00:19:13,020
Then she replied with a stream of English.

267
00:19:13,020 --> 00:19:15,020
I laughed along...

268
00:19:15,020 --> 00:19:16,520
...and she left.

269
00:19:16,520 --> 00:19:18,520
I wondered whether I had said something wrong.

270
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:22,520
What had she said? Maybe I should have explained myself more clearly.

271
00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:31,520
I asked the writer who spoke English, “What did that blonde woman say at the end?” In simple terms...

272
00:19:31,520 --> 00:19:37,520
...she had said, “So what?” “So what?”

273
00:19:37,520 --> 00:19:40,520
That was painful. How embarrassing.

274
00:19:40,520 --> 00:19:48,520
I had stopped her with broken English, only for her to decide I was talking nonsense and leave. It was funny, but also very sad.

275
00:19:48,520 --> 00:19:51,520
English was difficult.

276
00:19:51,520 --> 00:19:53,520
It's especially hard when you have to respond on the spot.

277
00:19:53,520 --> 00:19:56,520
I imagine English must have been difficult in New York too.

278
00:19:56,520 --> 00:20:00,520
In my case, though, my manager...

279
00:20:00,520 --> 00:20:04,520
...speaks English and is very sociable.

280
00:20:04,520 --> 00:20:05,520
That's excellent.

281
00:20:05,520 --> 00:20:12,520
She stayed with me the whole time. We went into shops together, and she helped whenever we were with the group.

282
00:20:12,520 --> 00:20:15,520
So I had no trouble at all. I see.

283
00:20:15,520 --> 00:20:19,520
At the autograph session, each of us was assigned an interpreter.

284
00:20:19,520 --> 00:20:20,520
They translated things for us.

285
00:20:20,520 --> 00:20:24,520
They went that far? Yes. Amazing.

286
00:20:24,520 --> 00:20:27,520
It made me think I really should learn to speak some English.

287
00:20:27,520 --> 00:20:29,520
Was the phrase “Have fun”?

288
00:20:29,520 --> 00:20:33,520
They taught me phrases like “Enjoy yourself.”

289
00:20:33,520 --> 00:20:38,520
I kept saying “Have fun” throughout the autograph session: “Thank you. Have fun.”

290
00:20:38,520 --> 00:20:44,520
Even learning one reliable phrase is valuable. I knew it was correct and wouldn't offend anyone.

291
00:20:44,520 --> 00:20:45,520
Right.

292
00:20:45,520 --> 00:20:51,520
On the way home, I visited a place called Mega Plaza.

293
00:20:51,520 --> 00:20:57,520
It's like Bangkok's equivalent of Nakano Broadway, with shops selling all kinds of toys.

294
00:20:57,520 --> 00:21:02,520
That sounds fun. They even sold genuine Famicom cartridges.

295
00:21:02,520 --> 00:21:07,520
They made me nostalgic, though they were priced accordingly.

296
00:21:07,520 --> 00:21:11,520
I also went to McDonald's. Of course you did.

297
00:21:11,520 --> 00:21:13,520
They had properly spicy chicken.

298
00:21:13,520 --> 00:21:14,520
They did?

299
00:21:14,520 --> 00:21:16,520
Yes, fried chicken.

300
00:21:16,520 --> 00:21:20,520
After enjoying all that, I had bought so much that my luggage was completely full.

301
00:21:20,520 --> 00:21:27,520
Because my flight was on a low-cost carrier, I had to reduce the number of bags.

302
00:21:27,520 --> 00:21:32,520
If you have too many, you pay an additional fee.

303
00:21:32,520 --> 00:21:38,520
I was surprised. Then the writer told me what was really an urban legend...

304
00:21:38,520 --> 00:21:47,520
...that if you fastened all your tote bags onto the suitcase...

305
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:48,520
Right...

306
00:21:48,520 --> 00:21:52,520
...and claimed it was one item, they might let you through.

307
00:21:52,520 --> 00:21:57,520
I learned afterward that he had been joking. Of course—there's no way that would work.

308
00:21:57,520 --> 00:22:04,520
He said it was a joke, but I believed him and bundled everything together anyway.

309
00:22:04,520 --> 00:22:09,520
I had three tote bags, making four pieces together with the suitcase.

310
00:22:09,520 --> 00:22:31,520
I hung all three bags from the suitcase's extending handle and nervously approached the baggage counter. I couldn't understand the staff's English, but trusted my friend. The agent checked my passport, carefully examined all the bags, and hesitated.

311
00:22:31,520 --> 00:22:46,520
I didn't understand her, so I had to rely on the few words I knew. Unsure whether my scheme would work, I said in a tiny voice, “One.”

312
00:22:46,520 --> 00:22:50,520
I said, “One.”

313
00:22:50,520 --> 00:22:56,520
The woman replied in an incredibly deep voice: “Four.”

314
00:22:56,520 --> 00:22:58,520
Sorry.

315
00:22:58,520 --> 00:23:02,520
I'll pay the excess-baggage fee. Naturally.

316
00:23:02,520 --> 00:23:08,520
I challenged her with nothing but that timid “one,” even though it obviously couldn't work.

317
00:23:08,520 --> 00:23:09,520
That was reckless.

318
00:23:09,520 --> 00:23:12,520
She must have thought, “This guy is confronting me head-on.” “Four.”

319
00:23:12,520 --> 00:23:16,520
“Four. Four.” I heard that word perfectly. Of course it failed.

320
00:23:16,520 --> 00:23:21,520
After those embarrassing experiences, I realized I need to study English properly.

321
00:23:21,520 --> 00:23:26,520
Yes, I thought so too. Still, it was an enjoyable trip.

322
00:23:26,520 --> 00:23:31,520
That's good. Something similar happened to me before my flight home, around the carry-on and security area...

323
00:23:31,520 --> 00:23:41,520
You go through a body scanner, like a metal detector: you stand inside the cylinder, raise both arms, it scans you, and they tell you to pass through.

324
00:23:41,520 --> 00:23:50,520
As I was leaving it, two women by the exit pointed at my feet and kept saying something.

325
00:23:50,520 --> 00:24:17,520
I couldn't understand, so I looked confused. After they repeated it two or three times, I finally caught the word “cute.” They had been saying they liked my shoes. Later I asked someone who spoke English whether they had wanted me to remove my shoes, and learned they were only complimenting them.

326
00:24:17,520 --> 00:24:30,520
Had I understood, I could have replied enthusiastically. Instead I could only stare anxiously. So I also felt that I should learn at least some English.

327
00:24:31,520 --> 00:24:49,520
Exactly. And perhaps someday Hondo Landing Operation might hold a public recording overseas.

328
00:24:49,520 --> 00:25:00,520
Then let's skip the group dinner and have the four of us travel abroad together. Absolutely not. Definitely not? Fine, forget it.

329
00:25:00,520 --> 00:25:01,520
Why not Thailand?

330
00:25:01,520 --> 00:25:28,520
The food is genuinely excellent. Now I'm craving Thai food. There's gapao rice, pad thai, and plenty more—provided you can eat cilantro. I can't.

331
00:25:28,520 --> 00:25:29,520
Then...

332
00:25:31,520 --> 00:25:33,520
What about the director?

333
00:25:33,520 --> 00:25:36,520
Can you eat cilantro? Yes. Why are you grimacing?

334
00:25:36,520 --> 00:25:37,520
Good, good.

335
00:25:37,520 --> 00:25:38,520
That's a relief.

336
00:25:38,520 --> 00:25:42,520
Then the two of you can go. I'm glad he genuinely likes cilantro, because I didn't want that answer to be merely playing along with the joke.

337
00:25:42,520 --> 00:25:44,520
He really does like it. We should do this sometime.

338
00:25:44,520 --> 00:25:48,520
That “yeah” was cute. There are even restaurants specializing in cilantro, so let's go sometime.

339
00:25:48,520 --> 00:25:53,520
You two go. Use Chouseisan—add it to the scheduling poll. Right, we have to do that.

340
00:25:53,520 --> 00:25:59,520
We need to complete the poll. Do it properly for October. I can't today, but I'll do it later. Please do.

341
00:25:59,520 --> 00:26:00,520
Okay.

342
00:26:00,520 --> 00:26:04,520
As long as you do it before October. Now it's time for the ending.

343
00:26:04,520 --> 00:26:08,520
We're waiting for messages from everyone listening.

344
00:26:08,520 --> 00:26:15,520
For postcards, write to postal code 105-8002, Bunka Broadcasting, “Kaede Hondo and Hantaro Tenshin's Hondo Landing Operation.”

345
00:26:15,520 --> 00:26:19,520
For email, write to sakusen@joqr.net, all in lowercase.

346
00:26:19,520 --> 00:26:23,520
You can also use the submission form on the program website.

347
00:26:23,520 --> 00:26:30,520
Send us a message and take part in this operation. The program's official X account is @hjsakusen.

348
00:26:30,520 --> 00:26:34,520
We post our photos and comments after each broadcast, so please follow us.

349
00:26:34,520 --> 00:26:41,520
Now for announcements. [Several work titles in the Japanese source are unclear.]

350
00:26:41,520 --> 00:26:56,520
Please check out those titles. On September 21, I'll hold a staged-reading event at Yoshimoto Makuhari Aeon Mall Theater.

351
00:26:56,520 --> 00:27:03,520
Our guests include Shuri Miyumi, Saho Shirasu, and Ebi-chan from the comedy duo Marie Marie.

352
00:27:03,520 --> 00:27:09,520
Another guest will join them, making four women who will perform the reading. [One name is unclear in the source.]

353
00:27:09,520 --> 00:27:25,520
Three writers—myself, Akamatsu Shin, who has written for Tales of the Unusual, and Onuki-san of Fufu no Jikan, formerly Takada Corporation—have each written a staged-reading piece. Please come if you can.

354
00:27:25,520 --> 00:27:34,520
If Hondo-san's schedule permits, may I invite her to an event like this? Gladly—if my schedule is free. Absolutely.

355
00:27:34,520 --> 00:27:45,520
We hold events like these and hope to do many more, both in Japan and overseas. That sounds great.

356
00:27:45,520 --> 00:27:47,520
Have you ever had someone respond, “So what?”

357
00:27:47,520 --> 00:27:48,520
No.

358
00:27:48,520 --> 00:27:49,520
[Laughter]

359
00:27:49,520 --> 00:27:50,520
No.

360
00:27:50,520 --> 00:27:51,520
Why not?

361
00:27:51,520 --> 00:27:52,520
That's too funny.

362
00:27:52,520 --> 00:27:53,520
Well, I'm glad the trip was enjoyable.

363
00:27:53,520 --> 00:27:56,520
How do you say `だからどうした` in English? “So what,” perhaps?

364
00:27:56,520 --> 00:27:59,520
[Laughter] What would it be? I honestly have no idea.

365
00:27:59,520 --> 00:28:08,520
Please look it up for us. Your hosts today were Kaede Hondo, who wants to travel overseas...

366
00:28:08,520 --> 00:28:10,520
...and Hantaro Tenshin, who wants to travel overseas too.

367
00:28:10,520 --> 00:28:13,520
This mission of Hondo Landing Operation is now complete.

368
00:28:13,520 --> 00:28:14,520
Complete!
